  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–72h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iranian FM Araghchi and US Special Envoy Witkoff held indirect talks on the sidelines of the UN General Assembly in New York on September 23–24 — the first confirmed Iran-US session since the Islamabad MOU collapsed in August. Araghchi presented a written seven-step road map offering phased reopening of the Strait of Hormuz within seven days in exchange for lifting the US naval blockade, releasing approximately $12 billion in frozen assets, waiving oil sanctions, and agreeing a wider regional ceasefire. Trump publicly characterized the proposal as "unacceptable" on September 26. By September 29, indirect talks had ended without agreement, and Secretary Rubio ordered the Iranian delegation out of New York. A senior US official stated September 28 that "there would be no agreement unless Iran's nuclear programme is addressed." → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> p=0.14 (post-UNGA collapse; re-engagement after formal expulsion structurally unlikely within 14 days)</p>

      <p>UKMTO reported on September 30 that three commercial vessels — including a crude oil tanker and an LNG carrier — were struck by projectiles in or near the Strait of Hormuz, consistent with IRGC maritime-denial operations documented throughout August–September 2026. The Strait's week-of-September-23–30 transit count was 119 vessels (approximately 17 per day, 7-day average), with over one-third running dark (AIS disabled). IMF PortWatch recorded one transit on September 27. The IRGC separately claimed the capture of a US unmanned underwater vehicle in the Strait in the same period. The Hormuz 30-vessels/day forecast (Sep 3–Oct 3 window) resolved as NO: no 7-day window in the full 30-day period approached the threshold. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> p=0.72 (IRGC cadence 1–3 incidents/week throughout Aug–Sep; 72h attack is modal expectation)</p>

      <p>The US Ambassador to the UN on October 2 publicly accused Iran of enriching uranium to 60% purity, citing updated intelligence assessments; the IAEA has not independently verified this claim. The US announced on October 1 the deployment of a third carrier strike group and a second Marine expeditionary unit — the largest US naval concentration of the current conflict cycle. Trump, in a September 27 Axios interview, stated "renewed strikes after the November 3 midterms are possible," framing the US midterm election as a pacing constraint on major offensive action. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> p=0.20 (presidential restraint framing through Nov 3 is the primary NO-weight; force posture and enrichment allegation preserve a rapid-strike option); moved <span class="cite">F6</span> p=0.07 (nuclear sequencing deadlock confirmed)</p>

      <p>Israeli election polls published October 1–2 show Eisenkot's Yashar party ahead of Netanyahu's Likud in four of five surveys, with the five-poll composite averaging Yashar at 22.2 seats and Likud at 21.2. The most recent Channel 13 poll (October 2) has Yashar at 21 seats and Likud at 19. One i24NEWS survey shows Likud ahead by a larger margin, constituting the sole outlier in the current polling set. Netanyahu's Likud filed disqualification petitions against both Arab parties with the Central Elections Committee, citing terrorism-support grounds; the ruling is pending. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> p=0.42→0.30 (DA opposing narrative accepted; Yashar leads in 4/5 polls; five-poll composite margin stable at +1.0 seat to Yashar); moved <span class="cite">F3</span> p=0.25 (Netanyahu bloc at ~54 seats vs. 61-seat governing threshold)</p>

      <p>The broader force-disposition picture as of October 3 — three US carrier strike groups, IRGC maintaining weekly commercial shipping attacks despite the blockade, and a complete freeze of direct diplomatic channels since the UNGA round collapsed on September 29 — describes a conflict that has, since the Islamabad MOU collapse on August 17, operated entirely within a military phase with no active diplomatic track. Iran's September 24 written road map, rejected by the US, was the last substantive diplomatic product of the UNGA round. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

