  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's public diplomatic posture in the September 24–29 UNGA round prioritized economic relief — lifting the blockade, releasing frozen assets, removing oil sanctions — as the first-phase deliverable, with nuclear talks sequenced to a second phase. This ordering is consistent with the Iranian leadership's calculation that the economy and the Hormuz closure are the acute pressure points: the second phase of the US naval blockade, active since July 14, has effectively cut off Iranian port access to the global economy. The Supreme Leader has not issued a public statement since mid-September; Iranian state media have characterized the failure of the New York talks as a US diplomatic failure rather than an Iranian retreat. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move (Iran's stated positions show no sequencing flexibility; no session scheduled before Oct 17); <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC's continued maritime-denial tempo — three ships struck September 30, one US UUV reportedly captured — documents the IRGC asserting operational capacity inside the blockade perimeter. The US Ambassador's October 2 allegation of 60% uranium enrichment would, if confirmed, place Iran at the same enrichment level it declared publicly in April 2021, consistent with IAEA reporting from the first half of 2026 documenting Iran's refusal to grant expanded inspector access during the conflict. Whether the IRGC's operational continuity is the product of a strategic decision to sustain military pressure through the US midterm cycle, or a consequence of failures in civilian-military coordination under wartime stress, is not determinable from open sources. The capture of the US UUV and the September 30 vessel strikes have not been officially confirmed by Tehran, consistent with Iran's practice of tactical deniability for below-threshold Hormuz operations. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span> p=0.72 without move (IRGC tempo 1–3 incidents/week; 72h attack modal expectation); <span class="context-only">context only</span> for <span class="cite">F4</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

